1092	sunday	new one. topics are transclusion and bidirectional linking; and purpose-built browser and mobile app.\n\nyesterday i realized that having the protolink/neolink configuration is super helpful for checking embed versioning. i think the protolinks are gonna have to be a tuple of the doc uri and the version hash. separately, there should be a separate field for the "parent" one. chat/comment functionality is too common to get rid of that or do some fucky thing to combine "parent" and "paleolinks" and try and make it elegant. there is the slight consideration of "do we want a document to have more than one parent". but implementing one parent, protolinks, and neolinks shouldn't be too challenging to implement with what we already have.\n\nfor transclusion, i think we should do something like python-style slices. in the embed, it would look something like\n\n`netdot/2323[234:904]`\n\nwhere the numbers inside are the character positions. i can't think of anything better. \n\nshould also probably have something for when someone wants to include the beginning of a document. like `netdoc/2323/[:904]` to show the first few sentences. i am opposed to doing the tail, because that could lead to abuse. maybe if you were a huge fan of shakespearean couplets or something.\n\nand then when you do a transclusion, it would update the protolinks and neolinks fields for those specific documents. this way of doing things doesn't handle nested transclusions currently but that can be figured out. \n\nfor browser stuff i am planning to package the electron app as the browser, and people can use it as an editor for things to conform to the schema locally and then upload stuff to the network. would use the better-sqlite3 npm package.\n\nwe also need to define what you can do with and without an account. i think viewing public docs is a good idea, but not any other actions. the browser should allow some offline functionality for not being logged in, like creating, updating, and retrieving, but not pushing to a server.\n\nalso, i want to make a react-native app that (for now) doesn't have any functionality besides doing push notifications. i want netaris to become my default messaging app soon, and if i am out and around i am not checking my safari browser and no one lets websites do push notifications.\n\nfolder grouping also needs to be a thing. (a folder is a netdoc with a title, a blank content field, and neolinks being the child documents?) if we can figure out how to get that down (and if we add voip), that is our discord killer.\n\nfor opening up a distributed netaris network, we are gonna have to have beta testers before going to market. we are just gonna fuck it up if we don't.\n\n---\n\ncontinuing from here because there was a big break. but i didn't go to sleep so it is staying in the same document. this is written waking up on sunday after midnight so monday.\n\n### versioning, linking, title changes\n\ni think i got what we need for the versioning and linking problems. \n\nthe solution is that each version of a document has the leading eight character b16 hash from the sha1sum hash of the title and content concatenated (no space or newline). this is very similar to what git uses, which uses the leading 7 (or more, depending on how big the repo is) characters as a uid on its own. this is chosen instead of a version number, because someone hosting on another service could go in and change their title or content but not their version id. the client can check if the title + content matches the hash prefix provided, and if it doesn't it can raise an alert "this didn't match". \n\nbut what if someone hosting on another service changes the hash to match? well, in the *protolinks* field, (turns out we don't need a tuple) we store the netdoc url with the hash prefix as a suffix. something like `netdoc/2323~ef89a033`. the slug doesn't have to be `~`, other options that are http safe include `-`, `_`, and `.`. and this is perfect because if someone wants to cite a *specific version* of a netdoc in their netdoc, they can use the version delimiter. if someone puts in a netdoc link without a version delimiter, the protolinks field will populate with the latest version hash prefix, but it automatically update that in the content field (it could mess up other people's splices). in the words of linus torvalds: "WE DO NOT BREAK USERSPACE!"\n\n### id system concepts\n\nthe other thing i have been pondering is an id system. i watched a 90 minute video of how urbit does their ids, and they have some strokes of brilliance but it is bogged down by trying to entrap it in an OS kernal. main takeaway: verify ids on etherium in batches to lower transaction costs. so here's what i'm thinking: \n\nnetaris does something where we mint private/public keys on etherium. once we get to a carrying capacity of requested ids, or like once a day (for when the network is starting out) (whichever is sooner), we do the batch transactions. so when a user signs up, they can be given like a little pseudoid where they can check out the network, post some things, but it is bound to the host. if they decide they want to, they can decide to buy a netaris id (netid) and then it is delivered to them as soon as the next batch transaction. \n\nthe idea is that you should be able to move your netaris id off the company servers and move it wherever, or log into other servers using your same netaris id. \n\n### futureproofing\n\ni really want to futureproof this network. i want this to be the network that humans on earth, LEO space stations, the moon, possibly mars, and maybe titan use for the rest of human history. also the cyborgs too, they'll probably use it. (i would like this network to be friendly to crawlers for the training data reasons i discussed two nights ago, but by god i do NOT want it to be "agentic". they can fuck right off). and to futureproof the netaris network, it needs to be set up that, once the standards are all finalized, netaris the company and hosting provider *could* die and dissolve and cease to exist, but the network can still persist. ideally the company will continue as long as it needs to to make *full network capture*. pretty much no one should be left making new content on https without netdocs and netids. but once the versions are finalized, it should be good to go to take on lives as its own. \n\nfor versioning, i think we should copy what urbit does, and use kelvin versioning. once the version gets to 0, that's it, it's done. for something like the linux kernal kelvin versioning doesn't make sense at all, it's too big for one human brain to hold in their mind *at once*. but for something like a doc schema, it absolutely does. so we should start at netdoc v9.99.\n\n### lazy id assigning\n\nthe other thing i was thinking about was *lazy id assigning* for documents. if you are in a group chat on twitter or discord, say, every single message you send has an id, and it is a really long ugly string. centrally hosted (company hosted) netdoc repos currently increment netdoc uris really fast because every message sent has an id. so what about having *lazy identifier assignment? a netdoc is only assigned a public uri *if we know the link to it has potential to be referenced elsewhere*. obviously, if it is linked to in another document or replied to or subscribed at all, it gets assigned an id. less obviously, *if the link to it is ever copied*, it gets assigned an id. someone might paste the link on their own machine and put the link back online later. this is fine because uris (literally "universal") are supposed to be public facing anyways. we can have a separate internal representation id that is long and ugly (that is not part of the schema, and is instead the host's choice) for child documents, and only assign it a netdoc public-facing b32 uri when we need to. this keeps links nice and pretty and conserves namespace. \n\nit has potential for abuse. there is nothing stopping people from installing a plugin that automatically copies every single message that comes through, that would make an id for every single one. but not everyone is gonna install one so lazy id assigning still conserves namespace.\n\n### extensions are already here\n\nas for open sourcing things, right now we are having a bit of scope creep on our local servers, but that is not a bad thing. right now, we have implemented or are about to implement \n- user preferences\n- user color\n- live editing aka cdrt\n- voice chat\n- lazy id assigning\n- caching documents (i mentioned this in yesterday's blurb)\n\nbut that is all extension stuff. the schema we give away shouldn't have that. \n\n### browser and ux\n\nthe browser at the beginning should have functionality that is nice to have that we limit the web version not having. i don't think the web version should render custom css (we can allow it for mobile devices for now, but not large screens). that should be a browser-only thing. imagine you are in the web version and you load a document and you can see `<style>` tags but it doesn't render, and then you get a popup saying "want to see the css? download the netaris browser!" that would be pretty convincing to me. \n\noffline viewing is also a feature i really want the browser to have. i can imagine a button "save for offline viewing" and it saves it in the local sqlite db. this would be different from downloading it as markdown. we might have to do something about loading markdown from your filesystem to the sqlitedb and vice versa. after all, netdocs are an sql schema right now. \n\ni also think drafting a netdoc on the web version should be allowed, but you have to make an account to publish anything.\n\n### progress report\n\ni also want to do a progress report at the end of each day. elon musk style. i think he was right about that one. today i: \n\n- figured out how to get the cdrt postgres extension installed in podman. once you get installed once, you can save it as a podman preset, so you don't have to install it again each time you rebuild the database. haven't implemented cdrt yet, but that will happen soon. \n- got started browser stuff. got claude to package an sqlite node package in the electron app, so people can have local editing when the browser is offline. \n- did a bug fix where markdown-style links were rendering improperly \n- did (but didn't push) a ui change where i changed the text buttons in netdocheader back to icon buttons. our investor yesterday suggested having download functionality. that means adding a download button. i also want to add a cache button. that's just too many buttons for text. moving to icons means we can bring back the share icon as well which is good for network capture. if we have cdrt indicators for who is currently editing, that's gonna take up space too that we can't have text there for. i'll comment a picture of what it looks like\n- separated the settings and login page, and added a big "download browser" to the login page for if you are on the web version. we should be pushing the browser as much as possible. added sun header to login page \n\n### planned \n\n- some ui changes with lines and colors and icons\n- the new netdoc button should be moved next to the new folder and search buttons. in its place should be "download browser" if you are not already in the electron browser\n- upload markdown functionality. also drag and drop file into sidebar would make it a new netdoc\n- inline markdown rendering in edit mode, like obsidian and vim	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81	2025-12-01 01:04:19.348+00	2025-12-01 22:12:10.492+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
