142	Francis Has A Netdoc	Nothing Much	ebbc7f08-a945-49e5-be6d-667f8b3fece4	2025-11-19 07:17:21.232+00	2025-11-19 07:17:21.232+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
144	Francis Has Opinions on Economics	In my opinion, neoclassical economics is not an ideal model of capitalism, it's an ideal model of *socialism*, I would argue really that classical economics (Smith, Marx, Ricardo) and Austrian economics is a much better *model of capitalism* than neoclassical economics, with utility theory, perfect competition, equilibrium prices is a model of socialism, to be precise a kind of utopian socialism.\n\nthe neoclassical revolution begins with Menger, Jevons and Walras, it's also called the marginalist revolution, since they believe in utility and find a way to price goods with marginal costs (if I recall correctly). the main thing is that they don't think that the price of a good derives from its way of production (which the classical economists take as an assumption). rather, it comes from my subjective valuation, the (marginal) utility I get from consuming another unit of the good. what's important to note is that the early marginalists, especially Walras, was a socialist, and thought that socialism and the free market guaranteed each other. Walras' model of equilibrium is something that he mathematically modelled with a central auctioneer who adjusts prices to make sure that all the goods clear. I remember that there's a theorem, I don't recall which exactly, proved in the early neoclassical era, that perfect competition is equivalent to perfect central planning/distribution\n\nthis crops up in the neoclassical theory of the firm. in the neoclassical theory of the firm, you abstract out everything from the firm, including change, innovation, time. one of the curious facts is that in the long run the profit of firms goes to zero (well, they have a paper profit, but it equals their opportunity costs). the assumption is that perfect competition is when everyone has perfect information and everyone has the same product and so on.\n\nwhat i'm coming to realize is that *capitalism works insofar as competition isn't perfect.* it's through the violation of perfect competition, through differentials of information and so on, that capitalism can function. that's where innovation, product differentiation and so on come from. well yes you do see theories of the firm that relax the assumptions of neoclassical economics, to study monopoly, say, but monopoly is seen as a relative loss of welfare as compared to the ideal counterpart, if anything there's a *gain of welfare* in a real-world sense because of monopoly (well, it's not all peaches and roses, and there are bad things with monopolies, but these bad things aren't in comparison with a strange utopian socialist schema)\n\nthis makes it feel quite perverse for me when economists say something like, "all models are false, some are useful, this is a useful model, perfect competition." maybe on a mathematical sense it's useful and for empirical problems you can add enough ad-hoc hypotheses or assumptions to get the numbers right, and in that way it's useful (though i think you could probably do the same with the labour theory of value or whatever if you had an entire school of economics doing that). because the only way that capitalism works is through these crises and breakdowns, but also the violation of perfect competition. it's "morally" (well, philosophically) wrong for me. in a hegelian sense i think that the imperfect deviation from the ideal has to be integrated back into the ideal model itself. and the truth is that the classical economists were perfectly able to do this, Smith and Ricardo understood competition as-it-exists-in-the-real-world well.\n\nAnwar Shaikh's thesis is that competition is a war of all against all and it's a dirty fight for profit, which firms do by doing things like cutting prices. he also points out that firms are not *price-takers* (they don't meeky take the price "determined by the market"). well there's a theory of price-makers, for imperfect competition, like monopoly, where price is determined by the quantity that you output, but notably the company does not have the formula  P(Q). he points out that firms are also going in blind and they have to make a bet essentially, by pricing their good at $10 or $15 or whatever. firms are not ambiently absorbing the price based on the vibe of the market, the vibe of the market and the price conventions of the market come from firms making informed risks and trying to calculate their expected profit\n\nhe calls this "real competition" and he says that it's studied by the classical economists, and that this was repressed by the marginalists like Walras. later, heterodox economists popped up who would annoy the mainstream economists by telling them how the real world deviates from their ideal models, and try to introduce irrational agents or what have you. and all to show how capitalism in the real world deviates from the ideal and so sucks in this or that way. but the heterodox economists are essentially in the same frame as the orthodox economists, the orthodox neoclassicals know very well that it's a useful model only and so on. but this makes both sides of them rather blind to the turbulence of the market in general, the main exceptions are the Austrian school with Hayek and Mises, who understand that competition is a process, there is no perfect information.\n\nthis real competition is equally repressed by neoclassical economics and utopian socialism both (which is why there were those like Lerner and Lange who tried to synthesise the two). especially Kantorovich, who came up with linear programming and attempted to plan the Soviet economy; linear programming crops up with "shadow prices" for accounting, and I understand that in the economics of optimization, even for the free market, if you want to find the efficient market prices, it's the same mathematically as Kantorovich's shadow prices for a totally planned economy on the Soviet mould (the Soviets had done a fair bit of reading up on neoclassical economics). from the point of view of the mathematics, there is no difference between the groping ("*tâtonnement*") of the market or a central computer. if anything the only way to critique this is either pragmatic or through the Austrian school. at the very least the classical school and the Sraffian school give you a more 'objective' cost-of-production theory of value, where the price of the commodity is the sum of the prices of the commodities and wages used to produce it, plus a uniform profit rate\n\nShaikh's argument is that these "prices of production" are real but that prices in the real world fluctuate stochastically around them, nevertheless there is a gravitational pull that these prices have. it makes sense, suppose I'm making a new kind of good, like the first iPhone; I can say that the cost to produce this was $x, and that i want a profit rate of about y%, but ultimately i have to make a guess, a risky move and then put in a price. and this can get stuck, because the economy as a system is path-dependent and not time-reversible and this will change the situation that other firms are coming in. but I am limited by the material constraints of the prices of my inputs (the prices of production)\n\nI mean why Shaikh is not a capitalist is that he thinks that there are recurrent crises of capitalism, and contra the Austrians you can't give them up by just foregoing the government and the central bank. he would though agree with Marx, but also Ricardo and Smith on this point, if I remember correctly. but it makes sense that a theory of the economy has to begin with the real dynamics that makes it work. you should start with fundamental assumptions like that path-dependence is real and that the only equilibrium you have is a statistical, stochastic one, and that firms are obsessed with making nonzero profit and holding onto it, and that the the economy works is by being turbulent and that this is also the cause of its immanent problems	ebbc7f08-a945-49e5-be6d-667f8b3fece4	2025-11-19 07:19:16.134+00	2025-11-19 07:19:16.134+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
