4z	ternary	so right now json is the internet's chosen representation of metadata, and it's actually pretty good. douglas crockford got that one right. it's inflexible. having no trailing commas was a *great* decision. something something basin. but now there is all sorts of weird stuff happening on the internet. crawly spidey bots and ai scrapers and things of that sort. have you heard of web4? the information going on the internet just isn't *structured* for json anymore. \n\nconsider a scenario where a data transmission is *unbounded*. we don't know how long this transmission is going to be. this is an underexplored problem domain, and beyond being theoretically interesting, it has a few cases where it would show up. these include edge compute for rovers, which could be exploring space, or caves, or the deep sea (and can't have a wired connection); or, if you had a distributed "AI" (sorry) training network that needed to send *really big* payloads to each other – like, model weights measured in hundreds of gigabytes, and that needed to be distributed inconspicuously because they trespass on bandwidth and maybe compute.\n\nso how do you denote data if you have a continuous bitstream, instead of a one with a pre-determined size, like a file? how do you denote where one field ends, and where one begins? can you denote more complicated datastreams, like repeating structures or nested structures? \n\nthese questions deal with *how do we pack data together in the best way* according to a couple criteria, in priority order. first, minimizing transmission size. second, how to ensure the data can be interpreted reliably. thirdly, how to pack this data in a computationally efficient way. to help imagine why these constraints may come up in practice, imagine a world where compute is cheap but transmission is expensive. like if you had supercomputers on different planets that had to send messages to each other across deep space. or, more close to home, if you had a distributed botnet across laptop-capability computers that need to inconspicuously need to get messages to each other (and not clog up the internet, which would be conspicuous). \n\nto get around this, you need something between a *minimally defined protocol* and a *maximally flexible specification* for data representation. that 2-2 contrast is not just for convenience and mnemonicism. a protocol is an agreement between two parties of how data is to be exchanged, and a specification defines how that data is structured. a good example of a minimal protocol is morse code, which had stop codes SK, AR, and VA, transmitted over two channels, for tone and silence. it may prove telling that the first time that humans had to make a protocol for data transmission had two channels for different functions and had clear delimiters. but this simple binary tree protocol [ [ meaning symbol, termination symbol ] space delimiter ] is not minimal enough for our purposes here, because by definition an unbounded data stream can't have *message* termination symbols. the obvious thing to do is just take out the termination symbols so you are just left with [ meaning symbol, delimiter ]. \n\nif your protocol has anything more than the *bare* minimum, it won't be able to handle an unbounded stream of arbitrary data. for a *bounded* transmission, you typically define all your [delimiters](/netdoc/2H) at the start of transmission in a header. it requires a top-level definition to determine how long the section for the *other* definitions is, basically necessitating a protocol with specificity by that alone. this disqualifies these techniques for our problem immediately, but we will still explore them because they are necessary for understanding the problem domain for "unbounded" better. \n\nbounded datastreams with a header can handle both fixed length and variable length fields, but either way, information for that has to go in the header too (deepening yourself into protocolspace). if the fields are fixed-length, you have to denote how long each chunk is and how many chunks there are. if the data chunks are variable, you will need to (1) define how long in each delimiter is (let's go with "length in bytes", and for fuck's sake let's keep these all these ones the same length) and (2) make sure that your delimiters for the arbitrary length data *aren't included in any of the arbitrary data you are encoding*. turtles all the way down. these definitions should take up a few *words*, which is enough to contain that as well as a version number and a "magic number" identifier for your protocol, you might as well have them if you're going to have a protocol. if you think your protocol is very good, you could skip the versioning and "freeze" it (like JSON, which was an extremely rare case where this was right the first time). \n\nlet's build our way up with an example that is an adaptation (or "improvement", if you like making things for the sake of making them) of one of the most common data formats in the world right now, jpeg. jpeg is an example of *even-length chunked data*. it uses a gradient for 8*8 pixel chunks, and these are the same chunks across every jpeg image. every jpeg interpreter in the world can rely on the jpeg encoding for the different chunks being the exact same so they can reconstruct the image. but here, we are going to explore *variable content*, *even-length chunked data*. \n\nthe jpeg chunks were chosen because they are (close enough to) the most common things that the human eye sees. but there are cases where one might want to use a different set of chunks. if someone had a lot of images sourced from the same subject, say, foliage, they might want chunks that better represent foliage. so if you had a data set of these chunks, you would be best transmitting these images by chunks and images all at once, for data savings in bits. define all your chunks one time, 64 bits each, and then send all your images encoded with that representation. this technique is advantageous if the size in bits of chunk defs plus image reps is less than however much it would have been to transmit these images with standard jpeg space. alternatively you could transmit the chunk definitions with each image but unless you have really high-definition images you are probably not going to save space.\n\nthe *arbitrary length data* is a more difficult problem. basically, you would have to look at your bitstream of all of the data you want to send in aggregate, after deciding what order. but let's focus on that particular bit for a second. assigning order is a question of its own. \n\nyou could assign order at random, which is not terrible. but there are also better ways to do that 	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81	2026-01-21 02:28:43.062038+00	2026-01-23 13:27:13.452+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
