117	Writings	1.\nWhistle-wind wakes me, wandering and wild,\nfrom the wonder-waste, where once as a child,\n\nso silent I sat, in specter-spun air,\nsevenfold songs of stillness struck me there.\n\nthose heart-proud plains prattle across the land,\npale pikes pierce the sky, stony pylons stand.\n\nbitter belief’s belly, broken and torn,\nthe barren blackened womb whence I was born,\n\nfirst fond I was of frost and forest tall,\nfair fate forced my hand, I fight and I fall,\n\nfrom hollow home she harkens me away,\nand harshly hastens the death of my stay,\n\nO, Colorado, claw me not back down!\ncrying kith and kin cannot keep me now! \n\n\n2.\nI overlook a gilded labyrinth\nA cacophony of glass glimmers and glints,\nAnd as I look from my iron plinth;\nThe shining of the city makes me squint.\n\nAtop the singing streets I can not think;\nTo chart the maze, I follow his blueprint.\nHe draws me by a thread and makes me drink,\nand in chaos, time takes a steely tint.\n\nRed madness crashes from the tenements,\nAs the sirens wail out a wordless hint\nThat I should dive from the embankment\nAnd light myself aflame, iron to flint.\n\nI pull my burning body from the dint;\nMy burning desires demand I sprint.\n\n\n\n\n\n3.\nA man came to me from the Web of Wyrd,\nThe warp and weft of fate drew us so near,\nAnd muses murmured softly, hardly heard,\nSo halted my heart and to it fed fear,\n\nFor in his arms once fallow fields now bloom,\nWith flowers fresh, as fair as they are frail,\nBut if fate lets loose a thread from her loom,\nThen lilac lovers' petals soon turn pale,\n\nThough silver shears could scissor free my soul,\nA single strand of silk seals our embrace,\nFor parts of him restitch old and torn folds,\nAs tender hands mend the tear with fine lace,\n\nFor just a moment, all my fears may fade,\nAs fate, so fickle, stays her flashing blade.\n\n\n\n\n\n4.\nI thought that I was strong enough to fend\nOff lovely Aphrodite’s soft intents,\nOver sweet drinks and silken compliments.\nI told the Fates that he was just a friend.\n\nAnd as we sang our lost loves’ last laments,\nOur hearts followed the sound down paths that led\nAway from faith and to lust in its stead,\nThen pierced ourselves with arrows Cupid sent.\n\nFor Atropos saw fit to cut two threads\nAnd tie remains together end from end,\nAnd though we tried, Fate forced our backs to bend,\nSo I fell from his arms onto his bed.\n\nThough when he tried, I refrained from his kiss;\nWhen her eyes shut, I threw myself to bliss.\n\n\n\n\n\n5.\nI blink and find his eyes are like a beast’s,\nAnd, looking down at me, he makes me weak;\nThe mirror of his eyes reflects a feast.\n\nHe senses in my scent something so meek,\nAn eagerness of flesh to meet his fangs,\nAnd waits for the confession that he seeks.\n\nHis harsh hands hold me still; my body hangs.\nHot jaws tear through the sinew of my skin,\nThe gnashing of my bones sings out with clangs.\n\nUnder his weight I feel my spirit thin;\nAs he cracks bones, leaving behind no trace,\nMy lustful laments drowned out in the din.\n\nBut in his eyes I again see my face,\nAnd this time find the beast is in my place.\n\n\n\n\n6.\nWhat map could plot the madness in my mind,\nA sweltering knot of sadness and sin,\nConfessions hidden, some cruel and some kind,\nCreep out from the cracks and creases within,\n \nBound to the alms and affections he spins,\nAre artful denials of affliction,\nAnd though his intentions and words run thin, \nI am so easily soothed by soft diction\n\nStill, songs of slow salt and fast spun fiction,\nFind solid footing when I see his face,\nSit by his side, and buy benediction,\nIn my burning bliss I finally find grace,\n\nI see past his words and yet I return –\nI throw my heart on him and let it burn.\n\n\n\n\n\n7.\nCan I not hear her words which flatter me?\nOr listen to what ghastly angels sing?\nFor when they whisper, "The bell tolls for thee,"\nI, locked in rigor, cannot hear it ring.\n\nUnbound by cold silk chains that hold me tight,\nThe specter in my mind drips from my eyes;\nMy hollow head gasps out in false delight,\nHeld in the ossuary of her thighs.\n\nBut warmth once lost cannot retake its place,\nNot while my heart has fallen to decay;\nThough undead passions call out for her face,\nThose echoes of our love have lost their sway.\n\nShe sings a soft song; my teeth pierce her skin,\nI echo back an empty groaning hymn.\n\n\n\n\n\n8.\nI fear you sense the stench of my transgression,\nHow far from graven trust that I have erred,\nDishonesty and trespass both are paired\nWith rotten lust beneath the confession.\n\nSo furtive, I shied from clean discretion,\nOnly memories of days we shared,\nHold back cleansing scorn I ought not be spared-\nFetid guilt lies beneath my profession.\n\nSo meekly you asked me if I had lied;\nthen, falling back, I stammered I did not,\nand sat a callous cretin before God.\n\nheaving my remnant love for you aside,\nrunning, panicked by your anger, I had thought\nthat I might soothe your heart with spoiled fraud...\n\n\n\n\n\n9.\nI held my split head up against the wall\nAnd as I sewed I thought I went insane\nI couldn't feel the thread of love at all\n\nAnd when I saw your face torn by a frown,\nPulled by a string, I made myself stand tall \nAnd played a puppet lover, guard, or clown\n\nBut even when I made your sorrows fade\nYou always thought it right to cut me down\nAnd searched for petty insults we could trade\n\nSo when I cried that I was leaving you \nYou sighed and split the seams that we had made\nAnd said for many years love wasn't true\n\nIf loveless years ran past with you in pain \nWhy did you wait and make me break the chain\n\n\n\n\n10.\nI pluck a lilac’s petals from their stem\nand drop them one by one onto his floor.\nI gently tug his sweater at the hem\nand count the moments till he shuts the door.\n\nI know the answer that I’m looking for\nis found below, haunting our lovely thoughts.\nA thousand times, and yet I ask once more:\ndoes he still love her? does he love me not?\n\nAnd though I heard the answer that I got,\nthat I am not alone within his heart,\nI want my love to fill his veins and clot,\narrest his heart - rupture the two apart.\n\nPain in my arm, the heart that breaks is mine.\nI tell the wilting petals I’ll be fine.\n\n\n\n\n\n11.\nAcross a sullen sky in old New York,\nA courtroom wrought from iron-guarded glass;\nAnd I, though carried by a steely stork,\nAm brought not as a babe but as a lass.\n\nA jealous heart is jailed by stories told,\nNow called forth by the judge yet unconcerned;\nDo I believe the story I have sold,\nAnd do I spend the silver wrongly earned?\n\nAnd is it not that in love all is fair,\nThat cities never sleep nor villains win?\nSo when I find what’s waiting for me there,\nWill my soul not pardon my heart for sin? \n\nOh, softly now, I speak my lover’s name,\nAnd tell the jury it’s all in the game.\n\n\n\n\n\n12.\nI bear a crying chorus on my back,\nand cut my teeth just so I hear them sing;\nbut in the din my skin grows thin and cracks.\nmy skull gives out an apathetic ring.\n\nAnd though administration sings my praise,\nTheir kind words land like slaps upon my face;\nAnother cup of coffee in my haze,\nFor compensation feels as fine as lace.\n\nI can no longer suffer as a saint,\nAnd carve my bone from flesh while children play;\nAs day by day my patience grows so faint,\nThough godless, I prostrate myself to pray.\n\nDeliver me from screaming angels’ reach -\nI can no longer bring myself to teach\n\n\n\n\n\n13.\nI found myself back where it all began, when\nI looked into the sky and wondered what\nwas causing knotted feelings in my gut,\nand asked myself if life was better then\n\nI slept in quiet comfort in the den\ndreaming I opened doors that fate had shut;\nI saw great tapestries behind them but\nDidn't notice fate pull her threads again\n\nwith each door I opened there was a cry\nlost souls cut loose by beasts I did not tame\nfell from my back and hit the earth below\n\nI took two sickly loves and watched them die\nand wrote that it was all part of the game,\nno looking back and nowhere left to go\n\nand everything stuck to me blew away\neverything I clung to, I blew away\n\n\n14.\nIm breathing in the ashes in the air \nthe wind fuels a bonfire of remains \nI'm looking past myself with a blank stare \nignoring my consciousness's refrain \n\ntelling myself stories, I entertain \nthe thought that maybe I can be redeemed \nweaving new threads and calling them my chains \npraying that its more complex than it seemed \n\nthe whistling wind of this land had once teemed \nwith lovely songs of things so fine and fair \nwhen my peers judge me I will be deemed \na howling mad beast caught in my own snare\n\n	0d962062-5c4f-4fc7-aa4b-a1cafd8bba4f	2025-11-19 07:04:19.217+00	2025-11-23 21:22:40.365+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
