1e	Onboarding v1	interactive documents (netdocs) are the perfect medium to channel the new internet.\n\nit's all connecting a network of heads. if everyone had telepathy, then we would have an internet of heads, and not an internet of wires. but very very few people are telepathic so the best we have now is an internet of wires. anyone can make their own internet! you know the whole cups tied to string thing, right? that thing that isn't really passed around from kid to kid, and is more an adult tells a kid to do it, so they try it twice and then never do it again for the rest of their childhood? well, string a few of those up, and make them connect like three people, right? that would be a pretty legendary childhood story, well, there you have an internet. if you and your friends strung a bunch of wires to eachother's houses and were all connected to them, that would be an internet. it is just much more convenient to use the pre-existing cable laid on top of it all.\n\nthere were the telegram cables, then there were the phone cables, and those were around for a while, they were routed by hand and then they were routed by machine, then came faxes, dial-up internet, and broadband internet. lately we had some cool stuff like towers, then 5G, and now starlink satellites. but completely parallel networks never really became a thing (not even milnet) because it's all too convenient to build on top of what's already there.\n\nthere are private networks in buildings where people have their offices called "intranets", but people go home at the end of the work day and aren't on that network all the time. and most people in residential buildings don't know eachother or maybe hate eachother and maybe some of them meet by chance and make friends but it's generally pretty rude to get involved in your floormate's life. intranets don't really go up in residential buildings (the original, original facebook was a rare one). but the scary thing is, a group of friends who live roughly in the same area who were really committed to it could have dug some cables underground and connected to eachother on it. this might be happening in some places, but we just wouldn't know about it because we are not on that network.\n\nor even scarier still, there could be a few people who are telepathic in the head and have all connected to eachother, and are operating as a hive mind. if this is the case right now, then the number of people in the hiveminds must be very small. anything more than, like, 5 people would be noticeable. there is just no possible way to obscure that.\n\nor even *worse*, a small group of people could have figured out how to clip out of physical reality entirely and hopped aboard an alien spaceship and moved to a completely different plane entirely. heaven's gate tried it. but if that ever really works it would probably be by a rogue group of mad scientists (like the ones who made covid).\n\nyou know those shots in *serial experiments lain*, where there are a bunch of wires on the poles, and there is a loud droning buzz? whoever wrote that show knew what was going on. at the ending (spoilers are okay on netaris) when lain was in a world that overlapped but did not intersect with the real world, in the world of "the wired"...\n\nbasically, the wires that span the world form a *substrate* that all the human organisms get online to. damn "the power of communication". damn "a super-intelligent organism". damn "the power of human ingenuity". you are on the internet now. we are doing something strange. we are building something that we don't quite know what part we play in, all of us. you are part of the *superstrate*.\n\nwelcome to the internet. the old internet, the new internet, whatever. you're reading this via HTTP, nerd. how long have you been online? how long has your longest internet break lasted you? i doubt very long. i doubt internet is healthy for a child's development, but from when you are a teen till at least 40, you are stuck on the internet. there is no escaping it. and god knows these cables are gonna lay around for a while longer. america could get partitioned like it does in *snow crash* but there will still be regional networks connected. hell, it could end up america's last great export is software (this is a software company right now) like it does in *snow crash*, and it is looking like it's tending towards that but by god i hope it doesn't go that way. you'll still be able to make phone calls. you nor your descendants will ever be offline. and yeah, a few people will raise their kids completely offline, but they're not gonna be offline all their lives. "welcome to the hotel california, you can check out any time you like, but you can never leave".\n\n"teleoplexy, or (self-reinforcing) cybernetic intensification, describes the wave-length of machines, escaping in the direction of extreme ultra-violet, among the cosmic rays. It correlates with complexity, connectivity, machinic compression, extropy, free energy dissipation, efficiency, intelligence, and operational capability, defining a gradient of absolute but obscure improvement that orients socioeconomic selection by market mechanisms, as expressed through measures of productivity, competitiveness, and capital asset value."\n\njoin us on netaris, the new internet.	00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000	2026-01-12 07:00:18.799323+00	2026-01-12 07:09:01.76+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
