2c	Solving the Cube	This is a beginner's guide to solving the **Rubik's Cube** using the layer-by-layer method. This approach is suitable for individuals with no prior experience and enables the solver to restore the cube to its solved state systematically.\n\n**Important foundational principles**  \nTwo key facts should be understood at the outset:\n\n- The **center pieces** are fixed relative to one another and define the color of each face. The face whose center is white will always remain the white face.\n- The cube is solved **layer by layer**, not face by face. Completing one layer simultaneously advances portions of the adjacent faces.\n\nThese principles reduce the problem to positioning and orienting the 20 movable pieces (12 edges and 8 corners), as the 6 centers remain stationary.\n\nIt is strongly recommended to begin each solve with the **white face** on the bottom (and yellow on top in the final layer). This convention aligns with most instructional resources and promotes consistency.\n\n**Notation**  \nCube rotations are denoted using standard Singmaster notation:  \n- **U** = Up face (clockwise 90°)  \n- **D** = Down face  \n- **L** = Left face  \n- **R** = Right face  \n- **F** = Front face  \n- **B** = Back face  \n\nA prime symbol (') indicates a counterclockwise 90° turn (e.g., R'). A '2' indicates a 180° turn (e.g., R2). All turns are performed as viewed facing the specified face.\n\n**Step 1: Form the white cross**  \nPosition the four white edge pieces to form a cross on the white face, ensuring that the adjacent colors on each edge match the corresponding side-center colors.\n\nCorrect example: white cross with properly aligned side colors.  \nIncorrect example: white cross with mismatched side colors.\n\nThis step is largely intuitive and relies on logical piece placement. Practice maneuvering edges into position without disrupting others. Specific sequences for common cases include:  \n- R  \n- F R  \n- F R U  \n\nA frequent configuration requiring correction (mismatched adjacent edges) can be resolved with:  \n**R' U' R U R'**\n\n**Step 2: Complete the first layer corners**  \nInsert the four white corner pieces to complete the white face (and first layer).\n\nCorrect placement aligns all three colors of each corner with the adjacent faces.  \n\nPosition the target corner piece in the bottom layer directly below its intended slot, then apply one of the following algorithms depending on the orientation of the white sticker:  \n- **F D F'**  \n- **R' D' R**  \n- **R' D R F D2 F'**  \n\nProcess corners in an order that displaces any incorrectly placed pieces in the top layer to the bottom for easier resolution. If a corner is already in the top layer but incorrectly oriented, use an algorithm to move it to the bottom layer and reinsert it correctly (e.g., **F D F'** or **R' D R F D F'**).\n\n**Step 3: Solve the second layer**  \nInvert the cube so that the white face is on the bottom and the yellow face is on top. Locate non-yellow edge pieces in the top layer and insert them into their correct positions in the middle layer.\n\nAlign the target edge to form an inverted "T" shape with the center of the target side face. Then apply:  \n- To move right: **U R U' R' U' F' U F**  \n- To move left: **U' L' U L U F U' F'**  \n\nIf no suitable "T" configuration is available (all middle-layer slots occupied incorrectly), perform one of the above algorithms on any top-layer edge to displace a piece to the top, then resolve normally.\n\n**Step 4: Form the yellow cross**  \nCreate a yellow cross on the top face (orientation of side colors is addressed in the next step).\n\nIdentify the current pattern:  \n- **Dot** (no yellow edges up)  \n- **L-shape** (or backwards L)  \n- **Line**  \n- **Cross** (already complete—proceed to Step 5)  \n\nHold the cube appropriately and apply:  \n- For backwards L (top-left): **F U R U' R' F'**  \n- For line (horizontal): **F R U R' U' F'**  \n\nFor a dot, apply both algorithms in sequence (in either order). Rare unsolvable patterns indicate an invalid cube state (refer to cube parity or disassembly information if encountered).\n\n**Step 5: Position the yellow edges**  \nAlign the yellow edge pieces so their side colors match the adjacent center pieces.\n\nRotate the top face until at least two edges are correctly aligned. Identify whether the remaining two misaligned edges are adjacent or opposite.\n\n- Adjacent: Position them at Front and Right, then apply **U R U R' U R U2 R'**  \n- Opposite: Apply the above algorithm twice (repositioning as needed after the first execution)  \n\nIf all edges are already correct, proceed to the next step.\n\n**Step 6: Position the yellow corners**  \nMove the yellow corner pieces to their correct positions (orientation is addressed in Step 7).\n\nIdentify any corner already in its correct location (colors match the three adjacent faces). There will be zero, one, or four such corners. (Two or three correct corners indicate an impossible state.)\n\nHold any correct corner (or any corner if none correct) at the FRU position (Front-Right-Up), then apply:  \n**U R U' L' U R' U' L**  \n\nRepeat as necessary (repositioning the correct corner to FRU each time) until all corners are correctly placed.\n\n**Step 7: Orient the yellow corners**  \nRotate each yellow corner so that the yellow sticker faces upward, completing the cube.\n\nHold an incorrectly oriented corner (yellow not on top) at the FRU position. Note the direction the yellow sticker faces (right or forward).\n\nApply the sequence **R' D' R D** repeatedly:  \n- Yellow facing right: apply twice  \n- Yellow facing forward: apply four times (equivalent to two full cycles)  \n\nAfter each corner is oriented, rotate only the top face (**U** or **U'**) to bring the next unsolved corner to FRU. Do not rotate the entire cube.\n\nThe bottom layers will temporarily appear scrambled during this process; this is expected and will resolve automatically upon completion of all corners.\n\nUpon finishing this step, the cube will be fully solved.\n\nCongratulations on completing the Rubik's Cube using the beginner layer-by-layer method. With practice, the process becomes significantly more efficient.	6aaca99e-5acc-4942-9c1a-5a37478b070f	2026-01-12 11:53:59.792968+00	2026-01-12 11:53:59.792968+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
