1173	VC Day 1	I landed in Philadelphia at 8 am. By 11 am, we had gotten acquainted, seen the unfortunately boring liberty bell, and had made plans to walk to my friend's coffee shop. The stroll was made a trek by the heat, humidity (an eastern feature you were not accustomed to), and by the assortment of bags we needed to lug around. By the time we arrived at the cafe you were gassed. We rested for a little and got pointed towards a nearby restaraunt where I could get a sandwich at. I got myself a vegan banh mi and some kind of weird blue tea and you just bought a (reusable) water bottle, claiming that you weren't hungry. While I was eating you were able to recuperate from the compounded effects of the climate, your lack of sleep, and what was probably the aftereffects of your somewhat recent surgery. The conversation picked back up as we sat in the air conditioned shade of the hipster vietnamese shop, and you resumed telling me about the lore of the assassin's creed games you had just marathoned. I asked if you wanted to see the river and you confessed that you were much too exhausted to do so. Thirty minutes and five triple a plots explained later, we got into an uber and riffed all the way back to the airport, where the vibecamp bus was to pick us up from. The only joke I have documented is "disappointed leg fetishist seeing a picture of a foot: its alright but it needs motion (mo' shin)." \n\nWe joined the crowd of campers awaiting the bus. At first we waited inside but as the time approached for the buses to arrive, the group slowly shuffled out the door. It was properly hot. Philly had reached 90F and according to the weather app, the humidity made it feel like 100F. I was dressed for the weather. You were not. After thirty minutes of standing sweltering, the buses arrived and we took the frontmost seats, hoping that we would get the most air. And we probably did, but it still was not enough to cool us down. As soon as the bus started moving you realized you were starving. I pulled out the snacks my mother forced upon me before my flight and your eyes widened like I had just informed you that you had won the lottery. I asked if you would be too tired for the camp and you guaranteed me that when we got close to the campsite that you would get a burst of energy and excitement. As you wolfed down the chocolate granola, the workshopped puns from earlier had been replaced by a delirious fit of pseudorandom giggles. The gameplay loop of the bus ride had turned into you pointing at a random sign or store out the window, doing some loose word association, and then giggling. I fell into the role of incredulous straightman, bewildered by the volume of bits and comments. One notable moment was when you saw a sign for Dover Beach and made fun of how silly Dover sounded and I had to point out the insane train of thought that had to look at the 4 signs in front of us, one of which was an arrow pointing towards "Assawoman Bay", and deciding that "Dover Beach" was the silliest. You smiled and ignored my comment and kept the bit about Dover Beach going. The entire ride I was struggling to keep up, as the stream of consciousness bits did not relent and I tried to acknowledge each one. It was very funny.\n\nYou were right about gaining energy. The fatigue from earlier in the day had vanished. When the bus arrived at our destination you burst out the door with vigor, excited to meet your online friends for the first time (Orb especially). I think this excitement and vigor characterized your behavior for the rest of vibecamp and the delirium from the bus ride had been pushed under the surface.\n\n	ce785309-d263-4cbe-ab9d-0950b3551d50	2026-01-08 02:44:13.561+00	2026-01-23 23:45:55.118+00	f	f	f	\N	f	f
