54	721	There are three major characters: Arvind Kumar, Mei Lin Iyer (née Lim) and Subramaniam Iyer. Arvind is a deracinated Singaporean bachelor in his early thirties, who was born in Thanjavur to lower-middle-class Hindu parents and came to Singapore when he was six. He is deracinated in the sense of speaking little Singlish except during NS reservist and being formal & stilted in Tamil while reading it well (results of having to formally study Tamil until he was 18). He went to ACS (Independent) for six years, did the IB, then went on to NUS. He has not settled down, mainly going through flings and casual sex with usually Chinese women after his dry spell ended after university. There is with him a chronic disappointment in himself, a tired frustration, an alienation from his family and his ACS schoolboy past.\n\nHe works for a company that is headquartered in California. He recently (perhaps 1-2 months ago) broke off a close friendship with his married university classmate Anjali, a half-Malayalee half-Tamil close friend. When Anjali became pregnant by her husband they realized it had become an emotional affair without either meaning to and he cut the emotional affair off to maintain boundaries.\n\nMei Lin was born Singapore Chinese, raised in Katong, and went to MGS for secondary school and then ACS (Independent) for IB (like Arvind, but different years). She comes from a well-off Straits Chinese Methodist family. She went to UCLA for university and rooted herself in the US, and now speaks in a strong California accent. She has not been to Singapore for extended periods of time since she graduated from ACS(I). She is married to Subramaniam Iyer, born in Edison, New Jersey. Subramaniam is a rich Tamil Brahmin now working in the Bay. Her parents' worry hinges on religion, that she is no longer churchgoing in the US & married to a Hindu.\n\nHis casteism is not the old religious feeling but a privately held belief (shared with right-wing white, Jewish and Tamil Brahmin colleagues) that Tamil Brahmin endogamy has eugenically bred for intelligence and this is the reason for Tamil Brahmin dominance in the Valley. He believes that Silicon Valley has shown that there is a genetic elite comprised of Northern Europeans, Ashkenazi Jews, Tamil Brahmins and East Asians and interbreeding between them is good on eugenic grounds. He privately thinks that American immigration should only be gated to those groups; he privately believes that H1-Bs and green cards should be barred from Indians except for Tamil Brahmins and maybe other selected groups to reduce Indian immigration, and has been doing quiet political advocacy for it among his business circles in the US. He has been feeding less incendiary versions of this thesis to a friendly Democratic senator who does not know what Subramaniam's aiming at. He keeps these sentiments to himself and is engaged in private amateur research in human biodiversity. He believes in Gregory Cochran et al's claim (introduced to him by a right-wing Jewish VC friend) that Jewish history had a selection effect on Ashkenazi IQ and wants to prove an analogue for Brahmins, who he sees as an embattled creative minority pushed down by the resentful unproductive masses. He is careful not to discuss this with anyone who do not already agree, so even his wife does not know. \n\nHis two secrets are that he was pre-marriage a secret donor to a legally dodgy boutique sperm bank and is carrying on an on-off affair (really more of a "sugar daddy") with a right-wing white sorority girl Chelsea who is still in college. He works at Arvind's company's HQ in California and has decided to take a six-month period at the Singapore office with his wife. He has come to Singapore with Mei Lin before, but only for weekends, at most a week. He thinks at first that Arvind might be a fellow Tamil Brahmin (based on his being in tech and being vegetarian) and attempts to figure out his caste background and is disappointed when he doesn't respond as a Brahmin would, confirming he isn't a Brahmin.\n\nHe has a patronizing contempt for the Singaporeans at the local office (since Singapore comes below the US in his ranking), and especially Singaporean Indians, who he condescends to in a friendly way. It comes from reading that Singaporean Tamils are descended from laborers and indentured servants imported by the British, and that they gave up caste distinctions by the 70s. He basically thinks that the local Indians are dysgenic and living on the sufferance of Chinese (because he accepts Lee Kwan Yew's eugenics-coded statements) except for the "brown sahibs" brought by the British and injections of Indian immigrants. To him the abolition of caste distinctions except among recent immigrants means he cannot eugenically place anyone. It helps that some of this new SG Chinese colleagues quietly collude with him here. This makes him suspect local Indians are less eugenically fit and intelligent and prefer recent immigrants like Arvind (Arvind does not know about this). He sometimes lapses into Hinglish that confuses the local Indians (since they don't know Hindi), though he gets along excellently with local well-off Singaporean Chinese.\n\nHe is condescending towards Arvind, who is notably not a Tamil Brahmin like himself, or even a respectable Nattukottai Chettiars (who he thinks are useful since he thinks they are genetically adapted for commerce, though obviously not for abstract intellectual thinking) but a lesser rural Chettiar sub-caste, something to do with oil. But he grudgingly agrees that Arvind is "good enough" despite his genetic origin since he's at least heard of Slate Star Codex (Arvind's vegetarianism is really for EA reasons). (For Subramaniam his taxonomy is less about UC/OBC in the Indian sense and more "do I have a evolutionary just-so story for their background" since he's reverse-engineering casteism in America out of evolutionary psychology which he does not realize is bizarre to Indians) For his own curiosity he grabs a few strands from hair from Arvind's desk and mails it to the US for genomic testing, in part since they don't have a Vaniya Chettiar genome on file. It annoys that the lack of direct connection to India make it harder to map out the stock of everyone. Arvind himself cannot really disagree with Subramaniam's judgement of him, not immediately aware its a genetics judgement. Mei Lin, who has loved Subramaniam more than he has her, begins feeling alienated in Singapore from her childhood and family and her husband's new side. She finds herself resisting dinners with her family, coming to church, meeting old friends. And she misses Subramaniam's affection to her.\n\nShe sees Subramaniam's phone lighting up with a message from "Chels" (it's a text from Chelsea, who she does not know about, but she has had suspicions). She doesn’t confront Subramaniam or confirm if it is real. It becomes the reason she first seduces Arvind. He seduces her next a week later. Arvind and Mei Lin start having an affair right under Subramaniam's nose. It becomes wilder in a way that begins to bemuse and then tire out and then worry Arvind. She also starts to spend more time with her family, starting to attend services. He stops drinking, begins reading again, and dusts off numbers of old friends.\n\nHe comes to a church service with her once at her old church when she invited him, sitting separately, without her family. He finds himself remembering hymns from ACS Monday chapels. Mei Lin starts to secretly hold onto a secret, "ironic" racism directed at Indians, first starting at her husband, who had molded her to become the perfect cosmopolitan Tamil Brahmin wife. She tells Arvind about it, about dealing with his family, and it curdles into contempt. She resents having been molded to be made acceptable to them. And how in Singapore she was told that caste was for backward FOBs but Subramaniam's business runs through caste networks. And the fact that he and his family believe that they're better than everyone metaphysically, which a haughty "No, you're *just* Indian.", that he's not at the top of the hierarchy and knows this. She starts wamting to furiously tell Subramaniam that he's lower on the totem pole than her.\n\nThe contempt becomes racism and at times accidentally spills over toward Arvind to her embarrassment, becoming the disavowed fuel of the affair (even as she knows the racism is wrong) That is when she starts to love him, though without wanting to recognize it, that he flinches and that he stays. She starts giving blackly comic post-coital rants and monologues—about race, sex, religion, God, America, Singapore, literature, which amuses Arvind and makes him more affectionate toward her. He tells her she should write a novel. She stumbles one day onto Subramaniam's dog-eared copy of The Bell Curve. She pores over the annotations in bed with Arvind, both drunk and in their underwear, half-amused half-horrified. He works through the genetics while she looks over the mathematics of the statistics, boggling at the annotations.\n\nThe six months finish up. Arvind and Mei Lin don't sleep together in her last week but go out on walks, visit their old school ACS(I) together, marvel at the "the best is yet to be" motto from Browning's poem. They remember over coffee on campus that ACS's founder Bishop William Fitzjames Oldham was born in India, from Founder's Day presentations. She tells him she found about Subramaniam's discreet inquiries about polygenic IQ screening of embryos, from an email she secretly deleted from Subramaniam's laptop. It was from an IIT alumnus who splits between Palo Alto and Singapore & is founder of an embryo selection startup. She tells him though tears that she's terrified of having her future children screened and made to incubate them, even if the email was not about her and totally theoretical. She cries in his chest but refuses his help, finally has him go over the start-up's press releases & the science together. They visit temples he hasn't been to for years, where he introduces her as a colleague. He visits her childhood home and says hi to her ACS old boy father.  She introduces him as a fellow ACS alumnus like him and her, which invites her father's fraternal feeling. She decides on the last day that she despises and loves Arvind. They both know she won't leave Subramaniam and it comforts both.\n\nThe Iyers fly unceremoniously back to California. A month after Arvind breaks down and starts crying and realizes that he could finally feel hurt by Mei Lin and at this point loves her, and hates himself for his emptiness and his loving her and his hating her. A few months after the return to California, Subramaniam comes across concrete proof that Mei Lin had an affair when in Singapore (though he does not know with whom), a hotel receipt. He does not care because he just heard that one of his sperm samples was used by an Ashkenazi Jewish Ivy alumna (an unofficial email, since it's illegal). He decides that Mei Lin "must have" cheated with a white expat or a local Singaporean Chinese. He ends up driving over to stay with Chelsea for the night, with a thinner excuse than usual. In Singapore, Arvind visits Anjali, her husband Kavin and their newborn daughter Divya (the baby that had been the reason for him to cut off the emotional affair). He gives them appropriate gifts in the manner of a friend within boundaries, promises to attend the baby's Hindu ceremonies. He holds the baby for a while and decides he has to do something with his life. He attends a temple festival with his parents for the first time in years, decides to attend regularly. \n\nFinally he goes for a church service which really moves him to tears, at the church he went to Mei Lin with. He decides that he's really not religious but that he is a sinner, decides to put sex and dating behind him at least for now. He starts looking at the idea of perhaps leaving Singapore, considers his own feeling that his life has been a mediocre failure. That he could have done better, gone to UCLA or UPenn when he was 21 if not for his funding, parents, ADHD (he takes this as an excuse), like his ACS peers. The reminder of being confronted with his old dreams and resentments as a second-tier underachiever who had an affair. All the same he recognises that Mei Lin went to UCLA and isn't living all the much better for that, considers that if not for the eugenics he's not so different from Subramaniam. His resentment exhausts him. He does not return to church. He starts keeping a Word Doc planning a move to Boston—jobs and/or grad school—setting himself a timeline and starts sending out applications. It makes him feel hopeful for the future, braided with a tender love for Mei Lin. He reads the Saul Bellow and Philip Roth novels Mei Lin gifted him. Mei Lin begins to sketch out a novel titled "Grow Old Along With Me", a blackly comic series of vulgar monologues. She also secretly prepares a fund if and when she needs to bounce from the marriage, and begins studying what she can find on population genetics and IVF, from links to blogs Arvind shared. She begins attending a Methodist church in California, not for religion or faith but for the hymns which she takes to with a real and tender piety and love: for her family, for her home, for Arvind, and hopes like Pascal that faith will follow.	2025-11-20 03:34:59.328+00	null	ebbc7f08-a945-49e5-be6d-667f8b3fece4
