244	1108	here is the list of stylistic things that need to be done for netaris. \n\nbasically, the philosophy for netaris styling comes down to *everything is consistent* and *no stone left unturned*. even rarely-seen pages of the ui should look the same as everything else. the other thing is that we need to *save the user from their own bad taste*. this comes up in several places, most prominently in how we do user customization. \n\n### color\n\n- the colors need to be consistent with eachother (sic). right now we have a lot of different yellows floating around. i have done my best to try and simplify them with the --vars in reset.css, but i'm pretty sure there are still stray yellows around the codebase. \n- there needs to be a color language. heavier yellows need to signify important stuff, higher saturation yellows need to be for really important stuff, low saturation yellows for not so important stuff. \n- h4-h6 need to be the same color as h1-h3\n- i am considering a discord-style thing where you can choose your own hue for the styles, or make it like fade rgb rainbow or something. but the discord model of selling css kinda sucks. this is also a case of "save the user from their own bad taste"\n- the default text hilite (sic) color needs to be different and probably also context dependent (editing, viewing, transcluding). note that the css to implement this varies by browser\n\n### sidebar\n\n- right now the documents that you scroll down on push the footer down if there are a lot of documents. this is bad, we need the footer to be fixed in place at the bottom \n- the "new netodoc" button needs to be moved to the top next to the folder icon and the search icon, and there needs to be an icon for it. the new netdoc button should also be a bit bigger than the other two and should have a glow\n- the wordmark for netaris should go beneath the sun on the webbrowsers (safari, chrome, firefox) but not the netbrowser (our browser). \n- it should say "netaris" in place of "netdocs" \n- there should be a "download browser" button fixed in place, very obvious, beneath the wordmark on the webbrowser but not the netbrowser. \n- the sidebar should collapsable if you are on the big screen, just like obsidian. but it wouldn't disappear, it would just be a thin thing on the side with the icons \n\n### icons\n\n- the folder icon should have a plus on it because it is make a new folder \n- same thing with the new netdoc icon \n- upload icon (download icon upside down staple shaped thing at the top). would go with sidebar icons\n- right now i had claude/gemini make all the icons. they look good, but they could all be made *slightly* more consistent with eachother. like with stroke width\n- (also color) the icon button outlines probably shouldn't use the main yellow like they do now \n- needs to be something for all the edit icons (edit/cancel, cache, save) to be stuck together. right now the button component has a fixed-outline, but i \nwould like there to be something where the three icons are in one rounded rectangle with borders seperating them, and maybe they collapse "save" and "cache" if you are not in edit mode. \n- need stylistically consistent icons for the sidebar footer\n- might be fun if the icons do a little animation when you hover over them (but that also might be too extra)\n\n### components\n\n- pages\n    - the titles for the pages (new netdoc, the other ones) need to be stylistically consistent\n    - need to decide whether permissions page should be fullscreen or not\n    - the dms header needs to be bigger and it needs a horizontal rule\n    - make the yellow horizontal rules in settings and profile gray instead of yellow \n    - the permissions admin page needs to be completely redone and made stylistically consistent with everything else\n- perhaps document embeds for the author could inherit the author's color. have tried it and i think it would look good in some contexts but not others\n- document embeds right now have the darker yellow outline, which violates the design language right now. embeds aren't like big parts of the ui\n- netdoc header/editor\n    - status indicator needs to change to a different yellow\n    - to be an obsidian killer, the build-in editor needs to be pretty good. i would like italics and bold to render with their underscores/asterisks, links to render with a different color and underlined (like vim), and headings to render big even while you are editing it. \n    - it needs to differentiate between if you have unsaved changes or not, in the status bar and if you click to exit with no changes and you have the setting turned on the popup won't come up. this will use a useState react hook\n     - should display publish time and most recent edit time\n- for the icon toolbar, it would be *really* cool if where you were hovering over the icon, the tooltip shows up in the exact same spot and doesn't move around (so you can actually read it), but there are two lines going from your cursor to the tooltip rectangle filling in a triangle (so you can see where your cursor is while reading it). \n- perpendicular lines for uploading media need to be changed\n- need to make a "customize toolbar" widget in the settings tab. there are a lot of buttons in the netdoc header, and some people might not want all of them to be there. some of them *need* to be there, and some of them do not. as a list they are\n    - required: edit, save, share, permissions, subscribe\n    - not required: history, cache, make available offline (netbrowser only, not implemented yet) \n- the confirm dialogue should make the background screen darker. also the animation where it comes in makes it not come into the right space\n\n### theme\n\n- right now the concept is "old yellow crt sulfur terminal". this is not something i want to deviate from. it is pretty close, but what would be *really* cool is if if like you could see the pixel lines like you would with an old terminal like that, and if there was a slight (1px, and not consistent, a partial outline) flickering glow around everything. it's good because technical (or just initiated) people get it when they see it, but non-technical people don't feel like they *don't* get it when they see it.\n- i think having a few featured official themes is a good idea, but getting the first theme down is the most important. but making other themes would be good for honing getting the main theme down right\n- we *could* support user-made custom themes, but this is a case of "save the user from their own bad taste"\n\n### interface\n\n- typography\n    - i think the default font weight needs to be a little thinner, and bold needs to be heavier. right now it is hard to tell them apart. this should be a simple fix\n    - this might be kind of difficult, but if we go with a monospaced concept, and *everything* aligns to the same grid as the font, that would be really cool (using css grid). it would make making a TUI much easier. different font sizes for headers kind of mess this up, but it *could* still work (make everything take up two character heights and then inside that change the size as needed)\n- animation: sidebar going in and out should animate. tabs swapping places should have a smooth animation. expanding the editor toolbar should animate. i don't think anything else should animate\n- disabled styling needs to be consistent (if you can't comment under a post, if you can't click certain buttons in the toolbar)\n- corner radiuses need to be consistent. for icon buttons they work, but i don't think we should include corner radiuses anywhere else. breaks the "old crt terminal" concept \n- buttons: right now the button shimmer does not move the same direction as the cursor. personally i think there shouldn't be any button shimmer at all, and it should just darken the button when hover over it, but button hover functionality is up to you \n\n### other \n\n\n- wordmark: we need a really good wordmark. i want to use custom font, custom design, not take a font from dafont.com, i don't want to use ai, not even for concept art. need a human \n- alerts and dialogues should not use the webbrowser default and should use custom components so as not to break the immersive experience. right now the copy link functionality breaks it\n- favicon: for the life of me i cannot figure out where the favicon is supposed to go. i've tried everywhere but it still renders the default vite icon. \n- sounds: probably a notification and ringtone sound. worth starting to think about now, but we should probably find a really good sound designer when we have more sounds we need than just two	2025-12-01 21:06:24.552+00	stylistic 2	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
181	1092	new one. topics are transclusion and bidirectional linking; and purpose-built browser and mobile app.\n\nyesterday i realized that having the protolink/neolink configuration is super helpful for checking embed versioning. i think the protolinks are gonna have to be a tuple of the doc uri and the version hash. separately, there should be a separate field for the "parent" one. chat/comment functionality is too common to get rid of that or do some fucky thing to combine "parent" and "paleolinks" and try and make it elegant. there is the slight consideration of "do we want a document to have more than one parent". but implementing one parent, protolinks, and neolinks shouldn't be too challenging to implement with what we already have. \n\nfor transclusion, i think we should do something like python-style slices. in the embed, it would look something like \n\n`netdot/2323[234:904]`\n\nwhere the numbers inside are the character positions. i can't think of anything better. \n\nshould also probably have something for when someone wants to include the beginning of a document. like `netdoc/2323/[:904]` to show the first few sentences. i am opposed to doing the tail, because that *could* lead to abuse. maybe if you were a huge fan of shakespearean couplets or something. \n\nand then when you do a transclusion, it would update the protolinks and neolinks fields for those specific documents. \n\ni started doing browser stuff. pretty cool. going to package the electron app as the browser, people *can* use it as an editor for things to conform to the schema locally and then upload stuff to the network. would use the `better-sqlite3` npm package. \n\nwe also need to define what you can do with and without an account. i think *viewing* public docs is a good idea, but not any other actions. the browser should allow some offline functionality for not being logged in, like creating, updating, and retrieving, but *not* pushing to a server. \n\nalso, i want to make a react-native app that (for now) doesn't have any functionality besides doing push notifications. i want netaris to become my default messaging app soon, and if i am out and around i am not checking my safari browser and no one lets websites do push notifications. \n\nfolder grouping also needs to be a thing. (a folder is a netdoc with a title, a blank content field, and neolinks being the child documents?) if we can figure out how to get that down (and if we add voip), that is our discord killer. \n\nfor opening up a distributed netaris network, we are gonna *have* to have beta testers before going to market. we are just gonna fuck it up if we don't.	2025-12-01 07:34:19.202+00	sunday	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
182	1083	web4, the new internet, the final internet should be set up well.\n\nhosting needs to be distributed and i think we can set that up early. the cool thing we have is our electron app looks exactly like a browser. the url bar at the top, when we ship it as an electron app, should have https://netar.is/... in the header. if someone else wants to set up their own server, it would be https:// whatever they want. earlier there was a conversation with a potential investor about "what if someone wants their own repo of notes, they should be able to edit it on their machine and post it on their server" i can totally imagine a power user wanting to set up a custom script personal wiki auto refresh blog with timing scripts to make sure the posts go live at exactly midnight at a certain day of the week etc. etc.. not everyone will use that, but some people will use that. it should be the same client, but it sends its request to a different server. anything that does not conform to the spec should get returned.\n\nfor onboarding, i think when you go to the login page, it should be the same login but also there should be a "download the browser" button. it doesn't matter that it's repackaged chromium for now, we can just call it a browser. i bet there is some clever stuff we can do anyways to get the chromium electron app smaller anyhow. when you go to https://netar.is in any browser that's not ours, perhaps we can have our server proxy to other servers if someone types in a different domain in the pseudourlbar, but it might not be worth it. we should probably encourage people to download the browser as much as possible anyways.\n\nwe have to change netdocs to being made in b32, and perhaps even url-safe b64. b64 looks ugly but it would guarantee futureproofing a lot of stuff.\n\nthe other thing is vanity urls. or, urls that are accessed by a name instead of a uri like a hash or a uid or something (netdocs currently use uids). the old internet uses name identifiers, but these have some problems, mostly with links breaking. for example, if you have document 1 with name A, and you rename it to name B, if you make document 2 use the old name A, the link points to the wrong document now. also, if you want to re-title your document (netdocs support this), your link doesn't update to match the new title. for these scenarios, you could have a redirect, but if you want to repurpose the old link to a new document, now you have to disambiguate between the now one that uses that name and the old one. is there an http response code for that?\n\nyou're gonna need something like that, either a vanity url or fetching the title, because you're probably gonna want to have some idea of what you getting before you click on it. the best potential solution i can think of is by default linked netdocs should fetch the title and render it, when possible, and optionally the name. right now linked netdocs render as an embed preview. it should definitely not always do that. people might want custom text for links like typical links are in html right now. i guess it can be up to the client (as like a setting preference) whether they just want the url to render as-is, or they want the title and optionally name to render too.\n\nit's not failproof. if someone changes the protodocument title to something else, it basically opens up spoofing. but that is on the user, if they decide they want their client to render the titles. or, in the protolinks field in the neodocument, it could store the document hash next to the url and the client specifically requests information about the document version with that hash and then it shows that one's title (if this is the case, than the document hash should be title and content together).\n\nthe other thing is, right now on the old internet, urls that serve files that are stored exactly as they are in a tree-based filesystem is really good for organization. different listings on a blog. different years for reports. RDF triples. more recent apps use subroutes for api calls. but i guess we'll have to hope that "2022 revised (real) weather report arizona" for every title isn't too much for netaris users to type out.\n\nso urls for documents is the domain header and then the netdoc delimiter and then the b32 uid. not super snazzy, but workable for the new internet.\n\nthere is also a problem about guaranteeing the content served is the same as it was when it was originally written. right now on the old internet anyone who has a website can just go in and change their stuff without saying they did, making history look different. "...the practice known, in what calls itself the real world, as “editing,” is considered unethical on a blog — and rightly so." ([C.Y.](https://www.unqualified-reservations.org/2007/10/how-dawkins-got-pwned-part-2/)) if everything is on the company hosting, than it's fine. edits are logged. but if other people are hosting their stuff, they can go in and edit. the best quick solution i can think of is a field in netdocs called "hash" that is a sha1sum (which, again, can be changed) and then people's netbrowsers (we need a proper name for the browser) can record the hash with the hostname+uid in their history, and then can verify the hash again on re-fetches. there can probably also be a distributed hash table.\n\nif each version is going to have their own hash, it can't hurt to keep version history clean. i keep finding myself toggling between the "edit" mode and "view" mode to look at my markdown rendering. maybe borrowing from git and have a "stage" button that locally stores your document and then you can click off and move around is a good idea, instead of making a version for every time you do that.\n\non a separate topic, the content field of a netdoc is literally just text. you can already put whatever the hell you want in there. markdown, html, css. it would just look as text by default. client-side, we have markdown rendering (pretty much). but if we sandbox html and css rendering, we can have that too no problem. it's literally html but better.\n\ndesign-wise, the text in the client is monospace. what if we had everything align to the monospace grid? it would basically make a TUI client very easy to extrapolate from there.\n\nalso, i really, really think everything should come down to just the two primitives, the document and the id. it would make distributing the network so it is actually a network, like, completely feasible. more on that later	2025-12-01 18:01:30.009+00	next one	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
183	1065	here is something of a plan for netaris. \n\nlast night i watched an interview from ilya sustkever, where he was talking about how he thought that we need new architectures for the machines. and in one moment, he censors himself as to what needs to be done to find the new architectures. the next few minutes after that, he spends a lot of time talking about brains. but if you put two and two together here, ilya thinks that to find the new architectures, he is going to have to do some mindboggling, very difficult, *skull-splitting* research.\n\nit's gonna make a lot of people pretty angry and upset if people go probing en masse probes into rats. but stuff like that, while good science and all, isn't exactly popular to publish stuff in current mainstream scientific journals. however, if there was a network where people could post documents decentralized... no, that's too far in the future.\n\nbut netaris doesn't have to encourage people to do experimentation to help solve these architectural problems. also - netaris is not made for these purposes, for advancing machine research or whatever, i am explaining this here as a happy side effect of what we are doing at netaris. here's how we're gonna save the internet.\n\nit's a very well known problem. the internet is siloed. it exists scattered across many people's servers - servers, the physical cabinets in warehouses, and servers, the computer programs that manage the inflow and outflow of information. and that would be... livable... if it wasn't for the more recent trend everything is breaking. you can't depend on microsoft to keep your operating system working, or cloudflare to keep DNS up, or AWS to keep hosting, or google cloud to keep hosting. let's take a closer look at the stack.\n\nthe "server programs" near the top of stack like apache and nginx are... actually fairly reliable and are well-tested and don't break so often. easier to go in there and fix because these aren't monolithic programs. but who knows if c will still be able to compile to whatever weird chips the US is going to have to hobble together after china indiscriminantly bombs formosa. but these are really only used for smaller apps. huge ones have all sorts of distributed load balancer complexity nightmares.\n\nthese live on top "operating system" point in the stack: why are you on windows 11? the problem for using windows on servers, is, well, if something isn't right for it, you can't really go in and change it, because it isn't open source, and the old ones go out of support eventually, and the new ones are programmed like shit. debian, redhat, and fedora work. it's messy, but it works. they still have the same issues of c/cpp not compiling.\n\nthese live in the "cloud hosting" part of the stack, and here's where it gets bad. if a cloud provider repeatedly shits out, well you have to change cloud providers. moving an entire app of decent complexity from one cloud hosting provider to another is at least a few days long task for one or two people to do (and, trust me, you want these teams to be small for tasks like these, otherwise you get insane overhead complexity communicating between people), and a complicated app like figma or something would be next to impossible to migrate off of AWS. everything has to stick together, and migrate around together. auth, storage, roles, hooks, elastic compute, all on these cloud services. very difficult, next to impossible to move around. if only there were some very easy coding building blocks, that would make it easy to move around, like documents and an id-system served from a purpose-built fileserver and database for that? no, that's the stuff of dreams...\n\nand these live in computer chips and ram and disks all packaged together in cabinets, computer chips that won't be made two years from now after china doesn't stick the landing and decides they don't need taiwan to make chips anyways so they take everything there and blow it all up. cabinets powered by electric grids strained to their limits by sam altman's folly.\n\nit's all breaking, half this stuff can't be relied on, and it's all siloed in. imagine you are an ai researcher and you can't see user habits or organic emergent patterns. the current messy internet architecture (that's all falling apart now anyways) i just described kind of shows the organic emergent user patterns, but not as well as one that has a bidirectional-linking transcluding architecture that anyone could extend and that has clearly defined id and document primitives (but that's too futuristic).\n\nit's not that there aren't labs sitting on top of stuff pretty close to this. is very ironic how meta is behind in the ai race. there are some comments to be made about organizational structure. from what i remember mark zuckerberg saying in one of his interviews, he has a core room of 30 or so people and they all decide what direction meta should take and they delegate from there, but the direction they put half their ai research talent to was making the infinite slop mill on instagram. so much for focusing on product. they had all the data they could have wanted to win - so much so that alexandr wang took it from them and sold it back to them. but really, meta had it perfect. they had a platform with facebook that other people were building mini-apps and mini-games on in the early 2010s like farmville and candy crush and shit like that, and they killed it. if they wanted to study organic emerging user habits and model the new ML architectures after that, they totally still can. "the age of scaling is over". but it's obviously stored in such a messy way that A.W. can siphon bits and pieces of it out, and it would be a real PITA to sift through.\n\nelon musk's xai, which is funded by elon's tesla profits, is sitting on top of x and they haven't done anything with that.\n\ngoogle is sitting on top of youtube and (if they still have it) of google+, but their edge is coming from their tpus and not from them doing clever stuff with their user data on social platforms.\n\nthe good thing that can be said about these companies is that they are burning money they already have.\n\nslightly down the "do we actually have the money to do this" scale is anthropic, which is backed by amazon, which does have money to burn.\n\nbut it's been some very misdirected efforts to channel the superconsciousness that have borrowed... hundreds of billions of dollars. famously, openai has borrowed just about everything, and has scrambled for more lines of credit, so much so that they recently secured a government bailout promise. they are going to go under and take everyone down with them.\n\nthese companies are all propping up nvdia, and when that pops, the music stops and everything goes to hell, unless google takes its place as atlas.\n\nnetaris is going to save everybody because our open architecture that lets people view emergent user patterns is going to solve the ML architecture (architecture in a different context) problems that will make ai work and able to do economically valuable tasks. our open id and doc primitives, combined with our purpose-built open server program and db program, with extensions, make it much easier to identify emergent user patterns, and its these emergent user patterns that are going to solve the ai architecture challenges that ilya was describing, and people can’t do that with their siloed platforms, probably bc it’s too complicated with the ad-hoc slap stuff on top of stuff way of doing app networks, but also increasingly apparent that if they haven’t figured out how to do so by now with hundreds of billions of compute then they are probably never gonna get it.\n\nI haven’t mentioned what i mean by “emergent organic user patterns” because i already have written about it at length, some of it (but not all of it) in this post, and i need to figure out how to reconcile the netaris network with my hypothesized emerging patterns. There have probably been arxiv researchers or whatever who have also looked at this\n\n/netdoc/1068	2025-12-01 18:01:50.314+00	working title	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
