261	1113	for minari\n\n# git\n\nthe gitflow is *atomic commits* on a separate branch and *squash merge* it. what this means is you do frequent commits so that way if you fuck something up you can go back quickly to something that was working, instead of spending more time trying to fix the fuckup and messing up other stuff in the process. the squash merge is very important to keep the commit history clean and compact. what this looks like in the command line is\n\n```\n$ git branch \n  master\n* style\n$ git checkout -b atomic # note the -b flag\nSwitched to a new branch 'atomic'\n$ git branch\n  master\n  style\n* atomic\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept # do this frequently\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept \n$ git reset --hard # use this command for if you fuck up something and you need to go back to the last commit\nHEAD is now at...\n$ git checkout style # note that there is no -b flag\n$ git merge --squash atomic # the --squash is the important part. please use squash\n$ git commit -m "this feature"\n$ git push \n$ git branch -D atomic # this deletes the old branch. only do this after you pushed\n```\n\nanother helpful command is if you accidentally stage changes in the wrong branch, you can use `git switch -c <branchname>` (note the `-c` flag instead of `-b`) to move it to a new branch (as opposed to `checkout`). \n\n# agents	2025-12-01 21:32:58.667+00	tooling guide	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
