258	1113	for minari\n\n# git\n\nthe gitflow recommends that you use *atomic commits* on a separate branch and *squash merge* it. what this means is you do frequent commits so that way if you fuck something up you can go back quickly to something that was working. the squash merge is very important to keep the commit history clean and compact. what this looks like in the command line is\n\n```\n$ git branch \n  master\n* style\n$ git checkout -b atomic # note the -b flag\nSwitched to a new branch 'atomic'\n$ git branch\n  master\n  style\n* atomic\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept # do this frequently\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept \n$ git reset --hard # use this command for if you fuck up something and you need to go back to the last commit\nHEAD is now at...\n$ git checkout style # note that there is no -b flag\n$ git merge --squash atomic # the --squash is the important part. please use squash\n$ git commit -m "this feature"\n$ git push \n$ git branch -D atomic # this deletes the old branch. only do this after you pushed\n```\n\nanother helpful command is if you accidentally stage changes in the wrong branch, you can use `git switch -c <branchname>` (note the `-c` flag instead of `-b`) to move it to a new branch (as opposed to `checkout`). \n\n# agents	2025-12-01 21:30:41.777+00	tooling guide	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
259	1113	for minari\n\n# git\n\nthe gitflow is *atomic commits* on a separate branch and *squash merge* it. what this means is you do frequent commits so that way if you fuck something up you can go back quickly to something that was working. the squash merge is very important to keep the commit history clean and compact. what this looks like in the command line is\n\n```\n$ git branch \n  master\n* style\n$ git checkout -b atomic # note the -b flag\nSwitched to a new branch 'atomic'\n$ git branch\n  master\n  style\n* atomic\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept # do this frequently\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept \n$ git reset --hard # use this command for if you fuck up something and you need to go back to the last commit\nHEAD is now at...\n$ git checkout style # note that there is no -b flag\n$ git merge --squash atomic # the --squash is the important part. please use squash\n$ git commit -m "this feature"\n$ git push \n$ git branch -D atomic # this deletes the old branch. only do this after you pushed\n```\n\nanother helpful command is if you accidentally stage changes in the wrong branch, you can use `git switch -c <branchname>` (note the `-c` flag instead of `-b`) to move it to a new branch (as opposed to `checkout`). \n\n# agents	2025-12-01 21:31:16.286+00	tooling guide	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
262	1113	for minari\n\n# git\n\nthe gitflow is *atomic commits* on a separate branch and *squash merge* it. what this means is you do frequent commits so that way if you fuck something up you can go back quickly to something that was working, instead of spending more time trying to fix the fuckup and messing up other stuff in the process. the squash merge does not merge all the atomic commits, it only merges one commit, and is very important to keep the commit history clean and compact. what this looks like in the command line is\n\n```\n$ git branch \n  master\n* style\n$ git checkout -b atomic # note the -b flag\nSwitched to a new branch 'atomic'\n$ git branch\n  master\n  style\n* atomic\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept # do this frequently\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept \n$ git reset --hard # use this command for if you fuck up something and you need to go back to the last commit\nHEAD is now at...\n$ git checkout style # note that there is no -b flag\n$ git merge --squash atomic # the --squash is the important part. please use squash\n$ git commit -m "this feature"\n$ git push \n$ git branch -D atomic # this deletes the old branch. only do this after you pushed\n```\n\nanother helpful command is if you accidentally stage changes in the wrong branch, you can use `git switch -c <branchname>` (note the `-c` flag instead of `-b`) to move it to a new branch (as opposed to `checkout`). \n\n# agents	2025-12-01 21:34:09.539+00	tooling guide	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
263	1113	for minari\n\n# git\n\nyou will be working on the style branch for now, and not master. the reason for this is there are a bunch of tests that only i know how to do that i haven't taken the time to write down yet, and i want to make sure nothing breaks. i am planning on giving you access to the master branch very soon. \n\nthe gitflow is *atomic commits* on a separate branch and *squash merge* it. what this means is you do frequent commits so that way if you fuck something up you can go back quickly to something that was working, instead of spending more time trying to fix the fuckup and messing up other stuff in the process. the squash merge does not merge all the atomic commits, it only merges one commit, and is very important to keep the commit history clean and compact. what this looks like in the command line is\n\n```\n$ git branch \n  master\n* style\n$ git checkout -b atomic # note the -b flag\nSwitched to a new branch 'atomic'\n$ git branch\n  master\n  style\n* atomic\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept # do this frequently\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept\n$ git add . \n$ git commit -m savept \n$ git reset --hard # use this command for if you fuck up something and you need to go back to the last commit\nHEAD is now at...\n$ git checkout style # note that there is no -b flag\n$ git merge --squash atomic # the --squash is the important part. please use squash\n$ git commit -m "this feature"\n$ git push \n$ git branch -D atomic # this deletes the old branch. only do this after you pushed\n```\n\nanother helpful command is if you accidentally stage changes in the wrong branch, you can use `git switch -c <branchname>` (note the `-c` flag instead of `-b`) to move it to a new branch (as opposed to `checkout`). \n\n# agents	2025-12-01 21:40:22.67+00	tooling guide	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
