338	2h	i have been finding some odd lexical gaps while making this app. i am going to plug a few of them here. \n\n- *delimiter*\n    - a sequence of one or more characters that separates two pieces of data\n    - this is an already filled lexical gap. see [wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delimiter) \n- *delineator* \n    - a single-character delimiter\n    - the most commonly used is `@`, and the only other main one is cashapp which uses `$`\n    - from the latin word *delineare* which means "to draw a line". using "delineator" for a multi-character delimiter would be inappropriate because multiple characters adds another dimension to a single point of data and that is not very "line". to run with the analogy, a single character would be a zero-dimensional point, and a string of characters would be a 1D line, so "line" would work for a multi-character string but it does not contain the language specificity so we are going with this as an analogy not a literal.\n    - this is not a previously filled lexical gap\n- *cartouche*\n    - a delimiter in an http path segment delimited by a preceding and succeeding slash `/`. \n    - from the french word for "cartridge" which napoleon's army used to describe the container-shaped symbols in egyptian hieroglyphs and became the standard term in egyptology for a containing symbol	2026-01-12 21:31:34.864+00	Terminology	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
