h segment delimited by a preceding and succeeding slash `/`. \n    - from the french word for "cartridge" which napoleon's army used to describe the container-shaped symbols in egyptian hieroglyphs and became the standard term in egyptology for a containing symbol	2026-01-13 20:50:03.609+00	Terminology	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
383	1108	here is the list of stylistic things that need to be done for netaris. \n\nbasically, the philosophy for netaris styling should come down to *everything is consistent* and *no stone left unturned*. even rarely-seen pages of the ui should look the same as everything else. the other thing is that we need to *save the user from their own bad taste*. this comes up in several places, most prominently in how we do user customization.\n\nright now the thing needed most is the theme we currently have, “old yellow phosphor crt terminal”, needs to be made stylistically consistent. separately, we will need at least a few more killer themes. i have no input on what these other themes should be. all of them should make people just go “wow” when they see them. it is probably best for development to focus primarily on the current theme but also do the other themes in parallel, so that way things don’t slip through the cracks while making them all. layout should be consistent across themes.\n\nthe rest of this document goes into the particulars about what needs to be done with the layout and this current theme to get it right. \n\n### color\n\n- the colors need to be consistent with eachother (sic). right now we have a lot of different yellows floating around. i have done my best to try and simplify them with the --vars in reset.css, but i'm pretty sure there are still stray yellows around the codebase. \n- there needs to be a color language. heavier yellows need to signify important stuff, higher saturation yellows need to be for really important stuff, low saturation yellows for not so important stuff. \n- h4-h6 need to be the same color as h1-h3\n- i am considering a discord-style thing where you can choose your own hue for the styles, or make it like fade rgb rainbow or something. but the discord model of selling css kinda sucks. this is also a case of "save the user from their own bad taste"\n- the default text hilite (sic) color needs to be different and probably also context dependent (editing, viewing, transcluding). note that the css to implement this varies by browser\n\n### sidebar\n\n- right now the documents that you scroll down on push the footer down if there are a lot of documents. this is bad, we need the footer to be fixed in place at the bottom \n- ~~the "new netdoc" button needs to be moved to the top next to the folder icon and the search icon, and there needs to be an icon for it. the new netdoc button should also be a bit bigger than the other two~~ and should have a glow\n- the wordmark for netaris should go beneath the sun on the webbrowsers (safari, chrome, firefox) but not the netbrowser (our browser). \n- there should be a "download browser" button fixed in place, very obvious, beneath the wordmark on the webbrowser but not the netbrowser. \n- ~~the sidebar should collapsable if you are on the big screen, just like obsidian.~~ but it wouldn't disappear, it would just be a thin thing on the side with the icons \n\n### icons\n\n- the folder icon should have a plus on it because it is make a new folder. same thing with the new netdoc icon \n- ~~upload icon (download icon upside down staple shaped thing at the top).~~ would go with sidebar icons\n- right now i had claude/gemini make all the icons. they look good, but they could all be made *slightly* more consistent with eachother. like with stroke width\n- ~~(also color) the icon button outlines probably shouldn't use the main yellow like they do now~~\n- ~~needs to be something for all the edit icons (edit/cancel, cache, save) to be stuck together. right now the button component has a fixed-outline, but we should try something where the three icons are in one rounded rectangle with borders separating them, and maybe they collapse "save" and "cache" if you are not in edit mode.~~\n- ~~need~~ stylistically consistent ~~icons for the sidebar footer~~\n- might be fun if the icons do a little animation when you hover over them (but that also might be too extra)\n- if you can figure out icon imports, that would be great. right now i am just hardcoding in duplicate icons. i think the issue was with making sure it would fit in the frame component. also ternaries (like the "subscribe" star) don't work in native svgs, so those would require duplicate files if they are imported.\n- for the icon toolbar, it would be *really* cool if where you were hovering over the icon, the tooltip shows up in the exact same spot and doesn't move around (so you can actually read it), but there are two lines going from your cursor to the tooltip rectangle filling in a triangle (so you can see where your cursor is while reading it). \n\n### components\n\n- pages\n    - the titles for the pages (new netdoc, the other ones) need to be stylistically consistent\n    - need to decide whether permissions page should be fullscreen or not\n    - the dms header needs to be bigger and it needs a horizontal rule\n    - make the yellow horizontal rules in settings and profile gray instead of yellow \n    - the permissions admin page needs to be completely redone and made stylistically consistent with everything else\n- embeds\n    - perhaps document embeds for the author could inherit the author's color. have tried it and i think it would look good in some contexts but not others\n    - ~~document embeds right now have the darker yellow outline, which violates the design language right now. embeds aren't like big parts of the ui~~\n    - there is supposed to be collapsed styling for headers and all that for embeds but that is only showing up in header, not the embed\n    - image embeds need to be able to refer to other sites\n- netdoc header/editor\n    - status indicator needs to change to a different yellow\n    - to be an obsidian killer, the build-in editor needs to be pretty good. i would like italics and bold to render with their underscores/asterisks, links to render with a different color and underlined (like vim), and headings to render big even while you are editing it. \n    - it needs to differentiate between if you have unsaved changes or not, in the status bar and if you click to exit with no changes and you have the setting turned on the popup won't come up. this will use a useState react hook\n     - should display publish time and most recent edit time\n     - need a wordcount indicator\n     - images don't embed properly. they render full size at the top of the viewer\n- ~~perpendicular lines for uploading media need to be changed~~\n- need to make a "customize toolbar" widget in the settings tab. there are a lot of buttons in the netdoc header, and some people might not want all of them to be there. some of them *need* to be there, and some of them do not. as a list they are\n    - required: edit, save, share, permissions, subscribe\n    - not required: history, cache, make available offline (netbrowser only, not implemented yet) \n- the confirm dialogue should make the background screen darker. also the animation where it comes in makes it not come into the right space\n- context menu: open in new tab, open in same tab, spell check, transclude if hilited (sic)\n- ~~new netdoc button in new tab gets squished and broken in mobile~~\n\n### theme\n\n- for the current main theme, it would be *really* cool if you could like see the scanlines like you would with an old crt display, and if there was a slight (1px, and not consistent, a partial outline) flickering glow around everything. \n- we *could* support user-made custom themes, but this is a case of "save the user from their own bad taste"\n\n### interface\n\n- typography\n    - i think the default font weight needs to be a little thinner, and bold needs to be heavier. right now it is hard to tell them apart. this should be a simple fix\n    - this might be kind of difficult, but if we go with a monospaced concept, and *everything* aligns to the same grid as the font, that would be really cool (using css grid). it would make making a TUI much easier. different font sizes for headers kind of mess this up, but it *could* still work (make everything take up two character heights and then inside that change the size as needed)\n- animation: sidebar going in and out should animate. tabs swapping places should have a smooth animation. expanding the editor toolbar should animate. i don't think anything else should animate\n- disabled styling needs to be consistent (if you can't comment under a post, if you can't click certain buttons in the toolbar)\n- corner radiuses need to be consistent. for icon buttons they work, but i don't think we should include corner radiuses anywhere else. breaks the "old crt terminal" concept \n- buttons: right now the button shimmer does not move the same direction as the cursor. personally i think there shouldn't be any button shimmer at all, and it should just darken the button when hover over it, but button hover functionality is up to the designer\n\n### other \n\n\n- wordmark: we need a really good wordmark. i want to use custom font, custom design, not take a font from dafont.com, i don't want to use ai, not even for concept art. need a human \n- alerts and dialogues should not use the webbrowser default and should use custom components so as not to break the immersive experience. right now the copy link functionality breaks it\n- favicon: for the life of me i cannot figure out where the favicon is supposed to go. i've tried everywhere but it still renders the default vite icon. \n- sounds: probably a notification and ringtone sound. worth starting to think about now, but we should probably find a really good sound designer when we have more sounds we need than just two\n- custom cursors? i like the old windows 95 cursors in general	2026-01-14 10:25:58.557+00	stylistic 2	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
349	2h	i have been finding some odd lexical gaps in computer-related things while making this app. i am going to plug a few of them here. \n\n- *determinative*\n    - from [wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinative): A determinative, also known as a taxogram or semagram, is an ideogram used to mark semantic categories of words in logographic scripts which helps to disambiguate interpretation. They have no direct counterpart in spoken language...\n    - "determinative" is the contemporary name for symbols used in egyptian hieroglyphs and cuneiform that would mark the type of whatever symbol followed. the same thing happens a lot with computer programming\n    - this has been chosen against the term from semiotics that best captures what we are going for here, "marker", as that is generally applicable to many things\n    - a determinative here is one or more symbols that separate two pieces of data or denote what type a piece of data is\n    - we are using this as our top-level category \n- *delimiter*\n    - a sequence of one or more characters that separates two pieces of data\n    - this is the only already-filled lexical gap in this list. see [wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delimiter) \n- *delineator* \n    - a single-character delimiter\n    - the most commonly used is `@`, and the only other main one is cashapp which uses `$`\n    - from the latin word *delineare* which means "to draw a line"\n    - this is not a previously filled lexical gap. using "delineator" for a multi-character delimiter would be inappropriate because multiple characters adds another dimension to a single point of data and that is not very "line". to run with the analogy, a single character would be a zero-dimensional point, and a string of characters would be a 1D line, so "line" would work for a multi-character string but it does not contain the language specificity so we are going with this as an analogy not a literal.\n- *cartouche*\n    - a delimiter in an http path segment delimited by a preceding and succeeding slash `/`. \n    - from the french word for "cartridge" which napoleon's army used to describe the container-shaped symbols in egyptian hieroglyphs and became the standard term in egyptology for a containing symbol	2026-01-13 20:50:28.947+00	Terminology	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
