405	1035	These are my thoughts based off common sense training. \n\nCommon sense training \nThere is a saying which I have encountered amongst managers and middle managers which will bother me. A leader can lead or manage anything. I assume the army in some way has assumed this attitude as the army approaches cadet education with a laziness that seems almost criminal. Cadets are not expected to have competent soldier skills, they are not respected amongst the greater army because the things they are taught are not relevant, and cadets are somehow exempted from tough training and the NECESSARY suffering which participation amongst the enlisted. Yet the army somehow currently fails the enlisted as well. The soldiers I have encountered from the New Jersey national guard have been lazy, stuck up, and perform their individual soldier skills worse than many cadets. The only soldiers who have impressed me were from the 82nd infantry, 10th mountain infantry, and the 11th airborne. The army right now cannot win a war, not for lack of arms, or any other logistical problem presented by a war in the pacific. \n\nThe army cannot win a war because its soldiers are now individuals, they do not work hard, and they do not have a do not appreciate that every man must be a rifleman. I, as a cadet alone, have heard the phrase that’s not my job, or worse, that wasn’t my job in the army. The story of Jessica lynch should be enough to stop these complaints and excuses which do nothing but create monuments to nothingness, but it doesn’t. \n\nI am horrified at the character and will to win of those around me. I cannot imagine how an army led by these people could ever win. I see my fellow cadets and think of training I did for the sports I participated in as a kid. I look at the training we have been provided and I think to myself no wonder their character and desire to win is as it is. Their training is of little interest to the officers and non commissioned officers who run it. The training has little to do with the creation of a competent young soldier, but with the creation of a paper pusher who wears the American flag. The only exception to this is CST and its focus on the animal phases. However, grading based upon leadership and intent, rather than, problem solving and results harms the value of the exercise.\n\nSo how does the army turn its officer corps around? How does the army make men and women who can lead training and inspire faith from their peers and subordinates.\n\n1. Terrain\n2. Woodcraft \n3. Endurance \n\nI’m not an expert - I do not actually know what is best. But I can look to history and see that every great officer had. And the ultimate common denominator is that the outdoors called to them.	2026-01-18 23:57:45.514+00	These	6cc2550b-c999-41b5-9e1c-85de93041ae0
