580	4z	so right now json is the internet's chosen representation of metadata, and it's actually pretty good. douglas crockford got that one right. it's inflexible. having no trailing commas was a *great* decision. something something basin. but now there is all sorts of weird stuff happening on the internet. crawly spidey bots and ai scrapers and things of that sort. have you heard of web4? the information going on the internet just isn't *structured* for json anymore. \n\nso how do you denote data if you have a continuous bitstream, instead of a one with a pre-determined size, like a file? how do you denote where one field ends, and where one begins? can you denote more complicated datastreams, like repeating structures or nested structures? \n\nthis is a question of *how do we pack data together in the best way* according to a couple criterion. first, minimizing transmission size. second, how to ensure the data can be interpreted reliably. thirdly, how to pack this data in a computationally efficient way. to help imagine why these constraints may come up in practice, imagine a world where compute is cheap but transmission is expensive. like if you had supercomputers on different planets that had to send messages to each other across deep space. or, more close to home, if you had a distributed botnet across laptop-capability computers that need to inconspicuously need to get messages to each other (and not clog up the internet, which would be conspicuous). \n\nyou could define all your [delimiters](/netdoc/2H) at the start of transmission. but this doesn't really get around the problem. it would require a top-level definition to determine how long the section for the *other* definitions is, basically necessitating a protocol by that one header. are the data chunks fixed length or variable length? either way, that has to go in the header too. if they're fixed, you need to denote how long each chunk is, and how many chunks there are. that should take up a few *words*, which is enough to contain that as well as a version number and a "magic number" identifier. if the data chunks are variable, you will need to (1) define how long in each delimiter is (let's go with "length in bytes") and (2) make sure that your delimiters for the arbitrary length data *aren't included in any of the arbitrary data you are encoding*. turtles all the way down. \n\nlet's build our way up with an example that is a marginal adaptation (or "improvement", if you like making things for the sake of making them) of one of the most common data formats in the world right now, jpeg. jpeg is an example of *even-length chunked data*. it uses a gradient for 8*8 pixel chunks, and these are the same chunks across every jpeg image. every jpeg interpreter in the world can rely on the jpeg encoding for the different chunks being the exact same so they can reconstruct the image. but here, we are going to explore *variable content*, *even-length chunked data*. \n\nthe jpeg chunks were chosen because they are (close enough to) the most common things that the human eye sees. but there are cases where one might want to use a different set of chunks. if someone had a lot of images sourced from the same subject, say, foliage, they might want chunks that better represent foliage. so if you had a data set of these chunks, you would be best transmitting these images by chunks and images all at once, for data savings in bits. define all your chunks one time, 64 bits each, and then send all your images encoded with that representation. this technique is advantageous if the size in bits of chunk defs plus image reps is less than however much it would have been to transmit these images with standard jpeg space. alternatively you could transmit the chunk definitions with each image but unless you have really high-definition images you are probably not going to save space.\n\nthe *arbitrary length data* is a more difficult. basically, you would have to look at your bitstream of all of the data you want to send in aggregate, after deciding what order. but let's focus on that particular bit for a second. assigning order is a question of its own. \n\nyou could assign order at random, which is not terrible. but there are also better ways to do that 	2026-01-23 12:00:40.862+00	ternary	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
