714	6a	to connect to netaris chats via an irc client, generally the requirements are: \n\n- server address: `netar.is:6667` or `netar.is:6697` (or slash instead of colon)\n    - it is highly recommended to use port 6697, as that one is encrypted and secure. 6667 is still supported for legacy clients, if they're still out there\n- login method: SASL (PLAIN)\n- username/Password\n\n## weechat\n\nweechat is particular in that it uses a slash instead of a colon to delineate the port. \n\n```\n/server add netaris netar.is/6697\n/set irc.server.netaris.tls on\n/set irc.server.netaris.tls_verify off\n/set irc.server.netaris.sasl_mechanism plain\n/set irc.server.netaris.sasl_username <username>\n/set irc.server.netaris.sasl_password <password>\n/connect netaris\n```\n\n## hexchat\n\n1. Click Add to create a new network, name it "Netaris"\n2. Click Edit, then edit the server to netar.is/6697\n3. Check "Use SSL for all the servers on this network"\n4. Set Login method to SASL (PLAIN)\n5. Enter your username and password\n\n## irssi\n\n```\n/network add -sasl_mechanism plain -sasl_username <username> -sasl_password <password> netaris\n/server add -tls -network netaris netar.is 6697\n/connect netaris\n```\n\n	2026-01-28 21:12:40.515+00	Connect to Netaris Chats via IRC Client	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
715	5d	building on xml in the big 2026 would be a fatal mistake. i refuse to do such a thing, so we will not be using xmpp\n\ni am working on a way to delimit an unbounded stream of arbitrary data. until that's complete, we will go with less ambitious models. anything more would be insufficient compared to the model i am working on. anything more would also lead to wasted dev time for no measurable impact on the user. \n\nso right now what i am working on is an irc-based **NETCHAT**. irc rfc 1459 is easy to implement. that is the one thing **NETCHAT** will claim full compatibility with. since netaris really is only operating off of one server right now, additional compatibility will include the following from IRCv3:\n\n- message ids\n- message tags, which are required for:\n    - replies\n    - emoji reactions\n\nand implementing its own things:\n\n- netdoc-specific extensions, likely using IRCv3 tags\n    - ability to "promote" message to netdoc\n\n---\n\n- differs\n    - irc has 9 char limit screenname, netaris has 15\n    - chatroom persistence\n    - a persistent user id\n    - we are omitting the [OPER command](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1459#section-4.1.5) because in the first version netchats there is only one chat admin\n    - PART and QUIT are both included, but functionally they are the same thing here\n- similarities\n    - Channels names are strings (beginning with a '&' or '#' character) of length up to 200 characters.  Apart from the the requirement that the first character being either '&' or '#'; the only restriction on a channel name is that it may not contain any spaces (' '), a control G (^G or ASCII 7), or a comma (',' which is used as a list item separator by the protocol).\n    - chatroom persistence\n    - a persistent user id\n	2026-01-28 21:13:26.971+00	netchats	ca82df4d-b69e-4506-89bb-f86531d07d81
